          %title
          %p This entry is about the book:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominique+Bourn"} Dominique Bourn
              %p
                %strong Mal’cev, protomodular, homological and semi-abelian categories
              %p
                Mathematics and Its Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 566
              %p Kluwer 2004
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4020-1962-3"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4020-1962-3
          %p
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+homological+algebra"} nonabelian homological algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing+lemmas"} diagram chasing lemmas
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mal%27cev+categories"} Mal'cev categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protomodular+categories"} protomodular categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-abelian+categories"} semi-abelian categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+categories"} homological categories
          %p From the introduction:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The most striking successes of category theory, as far as clarification of mathematical situations is concerned, are probably the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
              and the theory of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %p
              But up to recently, category theory did not provide any comparable insight in General Algebra, a domain in which element-based mathematics remains the slogan. In particular, category theory could not provide a structural tool able to grasp, even in the most representative category of classical algebra - namely, the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gp}
              of groups - the deep essence of the notion of normal
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %p
              And category theory could not grasp either the conceptual foundations of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing+lemmas+-+contents"} homological lemmas
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/snake+lemma"} Snake Lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gp}
              of groups, even if this category does not belong to the abelian setting in which these lemmas are generally proved in a significant categorical way.
            %p Of course, there have been since a long time attempts to provide an axiomatic context in which to get the isomorphism theorems, the decomposition theorems or the previous homological lemmas for the varieties of Universal Algebra: Baer (1947, [6]), Goldie (1952, [48]), Atiyah (1956, [5]), Higgins (1956, [54]), Kurosh (1959, [73]), Hilton-Ledermann (1960, [55]), Eckmann-Hilton (1962, [40]), Tsalenko (1967, [92]), but also Hofmannn (1960, [56]), Fröhlich (1961, [46]), Huq (1968, [57]), Gerstenhaber (1970, [47]), Burgin (1970, [34]), Orzech (1972, [83]).
            %p
              These first attempts, despite their interest, consist generally in a long list of axioms whose independence is certainly not clear. But more importantly, these axioms look desperately heavy and complicated in comparison with the elegance of the characterization of abelian models. We refer the reader to the introduction of the paper by Janelidze-Márki-Tholen (2002, [60]) for a reliable historical approach to this topic. … Establishing an organic and synthetic connection between all these attempts the ambition of this book. To achieve this, an additional ingredient was necessary, of purely categorical nature: the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+of+points"} fibration of points
